"""
# Interpreter: a user `fn generator(...)` shadows the lambda-based builtin

**Category:** Semantics / builtin dispatch
**Bug:** doc/08_tracking/bug/generator_identifier_collides_with_builtin_construct_name_2026-08-11.md

## Overview

The interpreter's builtin dispatch runs before user-function lookup, so any
call named `generator` hit the builtin `generator(lambda)` even when the
program (or the stdlib module `std.generator`) defines its own
`fn generator(initial, step_fn)`. The first argument is then not a lambda and
the call failed with `semantic: generator expects a lambda` — breaking the
exact usage documented in `src/lib/nogc_async_mut/generator.spl`.

The fix makes the builtin defer to a user-defined `generator` function; the
stdlib import pattern from the bug report is the repro, and a locally defined
`fn generator` is the generalization probe.
"""

use std.spec
use std.generator.{generator, generate_range, generate_repeat, take}

describe("user-defined generator shadows the builtin"):
    it("std.generator.generate_range and take work as documented"):
        val g = generate_range(0, 5)
        val xs = take(g, 3)
        assert_equal(xs.len(), 3)
        assert_equal(xs[0], 0)
        assert_equal(xs[2], 2)

    it("calls the user-defined generator function directly with a non-lambda first arg"):
        val counter = generator(10, fn(n): (n + 1, n < 13))
        val ys = take(counter, 3)
        assert_equal(ys.len(), 3)
        assert_equal(ys[0], 10)

    it("generate_repeat also routes through the user-defined generator"):
        val ones = take(generate_repeat(1), 4)
        assert_equal(ones.len(), 4)
        assert_equal(ones[3], 1)
